WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.775
Try it.

2
00:00:00.775 --> 00:00:04.151
Lab 1 runs each check
against a fictional fleet

3
00:00:04.151 --> 00:00:07.700
of nine devices and six
Autopilot registrations.

4
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.162
Here's step two, compliance coverage.

5
00:00:10.162 --> 00:00:14.871
Two policies reach no device: the
kiosk policy has only an exclusion,

6
00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:17.899
and an old Mac pilot has
no assignment at all.

7
00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:21.806
Two devices, a kiosk and a Mac,
have no policy reporting on them,

8
00:00:21.806 --> 00:00:25.712
so with the tenant's default
setting they count as compliant.

9
00:00:25.712 --> 00:00:29.398
And W S zero zero zero two
is flagged as ambiguous:

10
00:00:29.398 --> 00:00:34.374
two devices share that name, so a
status row could belong to either.

11
00:00:34.374 --> 00:00:37.688
Step five reads Autopilot
profile assignment,

12
00:00:37.688 --> 00:00:40.662
which is only in beta,
and the result says so.

13
00:00:40.662 --> 00:00:45.712
Of six registrations, two are assigned in
sync and four aren't: one out of sync,

14
00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.236
one failed on hardware requirements,

15
00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:51.575
one not assigned, and one
pending with no group tag.

16
00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:54.436
The lab repeats two checks in PowerShell,

17
00:00:54.436 --> 00:00:58.540
with the same results, and the
Intune tests pass: seventeen.
